<description>
Fill the rectangle from the lone foreground pixel to its nearest grid corner.

Input structure
- The grid is a uniform background field with exactly one foreground pixel.
- The foreground pixel's color is the color used for all added pixels.

Rule
- Find the grid corner with the smallest Manhattan distance to the foreground pixel.
- Recolor every pixel in the inclusive axis-aligned rectangle bounded by that
  corner and the foreground pixel to the foreground color.

Key insight
- The single pixel is one corner of the rectangle; the opposite corner is the
  nearest corner of the whole canvas.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "a single foreground pixel on a uniform background",
  "transform": "fill the inclusive rectangle between that pixel and its unique nearest grid corner with the pixel color"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 4-10 rows x 4-10 cols, uniform background (color 8)
  pixel:
    count: exactly 1 foreground pixel
    color: any non-8 color (observed 4, 6, 9)
    placement: must have a unique nearest corner by Manhattan distance (margin >= 1)
    position: 0-44% of the way from the nearest edge in each axis [observed]
  transform:
    rule: fill the axis-aligned rectangle from the pixel to its nearest grid corner with the pixel color
    rectangle: bounded inclusively by pixel position and nearest corner
    output_grid: same dimensions as input

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (task is simple enough for forward construction)
  steps:
    1. pick grid dims H in [4,10], W in [4,10]
    2. fill grid with background color 8
    3. pick a foreground color from {1-9} \ {8}
    4. pick a target corner (TL/TR/BL/BR)
    5. place pixel at (r, c) in the quadrant of that corner, ensuring unique nearest corner — concretely, pixel must be strictly closer to chosen corner than to all others (Manhattan margin >= 1); reject or re-pick if tied
    6. set grid[r][c] = foreground color
```
